# getpiggles.com — the Piggles account app. Copy to `.env` for local development.
#
# Most values are inherited from the repo root, which is why this file is short:
# the database, the Better Auth secret and the rest already reach this app. Only
# what MUST differ per app belongs here.

# ── THE ONE THAT MUST BE HERE ───────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# MUST point at THIS app. Better Auth derives its trusted origin from this
# value and answers "Invalid origin" to anything posted from a different one.
#
# The failure is worth understanding, because it is invisible and it has already
# cost hours:
#
#   • SIGN-UP keeps working. It runs in a server action, so the request never
#     leaves the server and carries no Origin header to check.
#   • SIGN-IN breaks. It is a fetch from the BROWSER, so it carries
#     `Origin: http://localhost:3021` — and if this variable is unset the
#     default is `http://localhost:3001`, which does not match.
#   • curl also keeps working, because curl sends no Origin header. So the
#     endpoint tests healthy from a terminal and fails in a browser.
#
# The result is an account that can be created and never signed into, with the
# UI reporting a wrong password. `apps/workbench/.env` carries the same warning;
# this app was written without an `.env` at all and walked straight into it.
BETTER_AUTH_URL="http://localhost:3021"

# Where the platform builds links for a PIGGLES tenant. `appOrigin(brand)` and
# `accountOrigin(brand)` in @sparx/links derive these names from the tenant's
# `platform_brand`, so adding a brand is configuration, not a code change.
#
# Both are needed and they are genuinely different hosts. `_APP_URL` is the
# operating console — where somebody already signed in is sent. `_ACCOUNT_URL`
# is this app, and it is what anything an UNAUTHENTICATED person clicks must
# resolve against: a team invitation lands on `/accept-invite`, which has to
# offer sign-in and sign-up, and mypiggles.com has neither.
#
# Outside production a missing value falls back to the brand's localhost port.
# In production it THROWS — a guess here mails somebody another company's URL.
PIGGLES_APP_URL="http://localhost:3022"
PIGGLES_ACCOUNT_URL="http://localhost:3021"

# ── WHICH BRAND THIS PROCESS IS ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# This app is the ONLY place a Piggles account is born, and it is its own
# deployment mounting its own Better Auth handler — so a signup arriving here
# belongs to Piggles by construction. Three things read it, none of which can
# work it out any other way:
#
#   • `tenants.platform_brand` on the new tenant. A Google signup runs inside
#     Better Auth's user-creation hook, BEFORE any tenant exists, so there is no
#     row to read the brand from. Unset, every social signup was stamped `sparx`
#     — and that column is what decides the branding of every email that tenant
#     ever receives, so the mistake compounds silently and forever.
#   • The free subdomain the tenant is minted with — `<slug>.piggles.site`
#     rather than `<slug>.sparx.zone`.
#   • The `issuer` in an authenticator app when somebody turns on two-step
#     verification. It is baked into the QR code and cannot be corrected after.
#
# Deliberately NOT read by api-rest or the event workers: those serve every
# brand from one process, so for them the answer varies per request and comes
# from the tenant's own column instead.
PLATFORM_BRAND="piggles"

# The identity this brand speaks with, read by @wizeworks/brand-core. Same names
# as the cluster configmap, here so local dev renders Piggles rather than sparx.
PIGGLES_BRAND_NAME="Piggles"
PIGGLES_BRAND_URL="http://localhost:3020"
PIGGLES_ZONE_DOMAIN="piggles.site"

# ── FURNISHING A NEW BUSINESS ───────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Onboarding names the business, then hands it to api-rest to be FURNISHED:
# every app switched on and seeded, the trade's config stamped, and the account
# filled with sample records so nothing opens empty.
#
# It is NOT best-effort. If these are unset or wrong, onboarding refuses to
# finish and says so — which is the point. The alternative is somebody landing
# in an empty workspace with no idea anything failed, because an empty workspace
# and a broken one look identical.
#
# The token must MATCH api-rest's SPARX_INTERNAL_FURNISH_TOKEN.
PIGGLES_API_REST_URL="http://localhost:3100"
SPARX_INTERNAL_FURNISH_TOKEN="dev-only-internal-furnish-token-change-me"
